Z||000002||ray b||12-28-2001||11:08 PM||||That tap handel better be caseharded not just soft steel or it will bust eazy<BR>I am breaking 1/2" drive 10mm chraptsman sockets like butter at only 60-80 ft lbs used a tork wrench need up over 100ftlb.<BR>need a real 4 side impactharded socket but will have to hunt a snap-on or industrial<BR>grade unit<BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||11
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||12-28-2001||11:18 PM||||When you find out let me know, I've struggled with vice grips  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||4
Z||000004||artherd||12-28-2001||11:33 PM||||CAREFUL, the ez-out will likely shatter at those torque loads (unless it's just enormous!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, wear eye goggles.<P>then try the (non impact) sockets ON AN IMPACT WRENCH.<P>It sounds crazy, but the impact wrenchs put very little side loads on things, unlike a long handle torque wrench.<P>I've impacted-out bolts that I would have snapped in half had I used a hand tool.<P>PS: PLAN on exploding 10mm sockets if you do this. you WILL send shrapnel flying! Protect yourself!  FULL FACE SHIELDS!<P><BR>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">      <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red.                     "Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver, In-Progreess.                                        -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||88CoupeV6||12-29-2001||12:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>CAREFUL, the ez-out will likely shatter at those torque loads (unless it's just enormous!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, wear eye goggles.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your not kidding. I broke one off in a broken bleeder on a rear 88 caliper. Expensive mistake. Could not be drilled out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>||||reg||8
Z||000006||ray b||12-29-2001||01:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88CoupeV6:<BR><B>When you find out let me know, I've struggled with vice grips   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>CAN'T EVEN SEE THE TOP to grab it, down a hole<BR>like every other time I try this<BR>EAZY OUT IS THE BIGEST sears had about 3/8 square on top at drive end but actualy 10mm<BR>is tighter fit than 3/8 <BR>WEIRD IDEA bolt+nuts to make a cheap adapter<BR>to join two sockets to get a 3/8 square hole driver with out paying snap-on $$ for a one use deal.top of socket is thicker anyway never busted one there, allways the open end goes.<BR>WHY DO I NEED the one thing nobody stocks?<P><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000007||avengador1||12-29-2001||06:08 PM||||You have to remember that the easy-out will tend to expand the metal as it is driven in. If you can shock it (by using an impact driver or wrench for example) you might get the broken bolt or whatever loosened. Make sure you use a good penetrating oil like Kroil. They say it will penetrate to one millionth of an inch gap. They also have it in a graphite formula to give extra lubrication. I literally took my car apart with this stuff.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||12-29-2001||06:50 PM||||You can buy 4 or 8 sided sockets at Sears||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||12-29-2001||06:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by avengador1:<BR><B>You have to remember that the easy-out will tend to expand the metal as it is driven in. If you can shock it (by using an impact driver or wrench for example) you might get the broken bolt or whatever loosened. Make sure you use a good penetrating oil like Kroil. They say it will penetrate to one millionth of an inch gap. They also have it in a graphite formula to give extra lubrication. I literally took my car apart with this stuff.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>wish I had air, can't even find my hand impact driver just used a hammer but still no luck, hate to pull the head for a bad plug<BR>4 broken sockets now and second eazy-out and counting<P><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fiero STS||12-29-2001||07:07 PM||||doesnt sears sell square drive sockets? another trick is to squeeze a nut onto the square end of the easy out. find a hardened nut probably 3/8. put nut and easy out in vise and press nut onto easy out then you can use a regular socket. i do this with taps quite often. hope that helps.||||reg||1
